d5fa43413c7cb2f8f26d1dec9.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099148 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099148 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ad5b795f-0907-55af-8029-2870075f1447" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099149 #edb/ref 1080 "images/42/422b9974766733b7e82eb36cec0080ad9c9aec1d69422ef1e79f74f2e780588c.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099149 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099149 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "109cc3e4-8f11-52ac-845d-bfc3e97932e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099150 #edb/ref 1080 "images/60/608c88c65eff4c63520a7a5a4a51a468601b0d6edcf5f063475c8429e1308962.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099150 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099150 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7787476d-bb7f-5cd4-b8fc-3eafa9ee0ef2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100638 #edb/ref 1069 "e-333" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100638 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of the function $f(x)$ is shown below. Sketch the graph of $f'(x)$.\n\n![](images/d9/d9746bb18a0e3ae662e477173e4da8b6599245edb02003287d83ab5e894aa814.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100638 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6ce8503-265f-5a02-93f4-ad99390c2734" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100638 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $f'(x)=0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x)>0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f'(x) < 0$.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at\n\n$$\nx \\approx 2\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\nf''(2)\\approx0\n$$\n\nand the tangent to the graph of $y=f'(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of $f'(2)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x)$, we can conclude that at\n\n$$\nx \\approx 2\n$$\n\nthe slope is negative.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y=f'(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a8f8c622296689efa9290c0ecb219abdba20cf71052ca77df1a131658d534a68.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100639 #edb/ref 1069 "e-334" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100639 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of the function $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown below. Sketch the graph of $y=f''(x)$.\n\n![](images/bf/bf8bde9f9b5060fbf14a329833f35ce9970c850e805e0c551cccaa70d6288c06.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100639 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "68f7ad36-cab7-5ffe-99d9-e720bbf7971a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100639 #edb/ref 1074 "First, if we denote\n\n$$\ng(x) = f'(x)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf''(x)=g'(x)\n$$\n\nSo, we need to sketch the graph of $y=g'(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x)$, as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the critical points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $g'(x)=0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $g'(x)>0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $g'(x) < 0$.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the critical points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=g(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $g$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{not extr.}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $g'$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at\n\n$$\nx \\approx 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx\\approx 2.25\n$$\n\nAt the inflection points, we have $g''(x)=0$, so the tangent to the graph of $y=g'(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis at\n\n$$\nx\\approx 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx\\approx 2.25\n$$\n\nWe don't know the exact value of $g'(1)$ and $g'(2.25)$, but from the graph of $y=g(x)$, we can conclude that at\n\n$$\nx \\approx 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx\\approx 2.25\n$$\n\nthe slope is zero and negative, respectively.\n\nTherefore, the graph of\n\n$$\ny=g'(x)=f''(x)\n$$\n\nmay look as shown below.\n\n![](images/78/785b221ae245b389e707f8189c91e57af888ecbb44dfce384ab1c60958d91a6f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100762 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6819" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100762 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ below. Sketch the graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$.\n\n![](images/1f/1fcbb5677ad7c8f630ccd7d194ef92f59fe3424d6bdb85e20f1ae522567b94ec.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100762 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98a83d94-0f65-5475-850e-c1abd8836e15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100762 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $f'(x)=0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x)>0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f'(x) < 0$.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x)$, we can conclude that there are no inflection points. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave up in $(-\\infty,\\infty)$. So, $f''(x) > 0$ and $y=f'(x)$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y=f'(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/15/1567dcdd3dac0eb6b76b71e20466a77457a3d956c85be96048e5d3a94568c03a.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100763 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6820" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100763 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ below. Sketch the graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$.\n\n![](images/ed/ed26d7560d9bd7294bf35a8e0e52292a7b5922d419303c63430c73c32c63070e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100763 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0b633f45-0fdb-5d4b-90e0-51c6628ce360" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100763 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x)>0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get $f'(x) < 0$.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $\\textrm{undef.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x)$, we can conclude that there are no inflection points. Also:\n\n- For $x \\in (-\\infty,-1)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is an increasing straight line. So, $y=f'(x)$ will be a positive constant on this interval.\n- For $x \\in (-1,\\infty)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave down. So, $f''(x) < 0$ and $y=f'(x)$ is decreasing on this interval.\n\nAdditionally, we have a rapid change of the slope of the tangent line when we pass through $x=-1$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=-1$, and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y=f'(x)$.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y=f'(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d00aadf7b1666ea8a762833d1844d638a224759c58d1b06929657ddb90dc4abb.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100825 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100825 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100825 #edb/ref 1039 "Sketching the Derivative of a Smooth Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100825 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100825 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3433cbc0-fe8c-5b67-a888-4df13b4b68c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100826 #edb/ref 1039 "Sketching the Second Derivative of a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100826 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100826 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e2ef7a61-79b9-5464-ac2a-3afb7dddd1c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100929 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100929 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100929 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100929 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100929 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100929 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100929 #edb/ref 1039 "Sketching the Derivative of a Quadratic Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100929 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100929 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fa025d7b-74d5-5627-a7cd-12e3681b8b82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1039 "Sketching the Derivative of a Piecewise-Differentiable Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "97710476-c327-5b2d-9675-10f36b967d3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1045 "Sketching the Derivative of a Function From the Function's Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186101604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "752a2e8d-dfc4-5210-8dfe-31f383092e9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101301 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101301 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20235" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101301 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a4/a44ff10b8b99077e742024dc360563e2481871a44592f1fd9260947dba82dfed.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y = f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101301 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "12ca6b93-de58-502b-8ecb-6b5fc6d679b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101304 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101304 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20250" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101304 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7c/7c816445b7d8f5e06780337062f260a965407a440b43b3542c640c44232c4574.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y = f(x)$ above. Of the following, which could be the graph of $y = f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101304 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50c220b6-4dcc-5cec-b906-7473e71c98bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101604 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101604 #edb/ref 1061 1224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101604 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "beaa6f10-10cb-5300-9425-d5fce50a2dec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101604 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186102004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101605 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101605 #edb/ref 1061 1225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101605 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1ebc76df-237a-5efb-96f2-b3585e2b318b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101605 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101606 #edb/ref 1061 1229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101606 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fe6a977a-c724-5c50-9d38-b0a7dc613ae3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101606 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101771 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101771 #edb/ref 1061 25278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101771 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "538a0daa-f570-5287-b6c1-2fd7946ab4da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101771 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101772 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101772 #edb/ref 1061 25279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101772 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "826cab52-c133-5e02-bcc7-a04d9190cd1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101772 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102004 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we are given the graph of the function $f(x)$, as shown below. The function has critical points at $x=-1$, $x=2$, and an inflection point at $x=0.5$.\nHow can we sketch the graph of the derivative $f'(x)$?\n\n![](images/b3/b3fcc7a08d2a083567cc2de62c9bb0c6e9516552c8a2f08d2e814604f5ec84f8.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $f'(x)=0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x) > 0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f'(x) < 0$.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nUsing this information, we can identify the regions where $f'(x)$ should cross the $x$-axis. According to the table, these are points $(-1,0)$ and $(2,0)$:\n\n![](images/e9/e98d21084e06d2a28509b0b68f449c5f42959ee1adad9b3dbf912885968a3b33.png)\n\nAdditionally, we are told that the function has an inflection point at $x = 0.5$. So $f''(0.5)=0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y=f'(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of $f'(0.5)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x)$, we can conclude that at $x = 0.5$ the slope is negative. Therefore, we get a point below the $x$-axis:\n\n![](images/59/5994a9139fbd1c530692a2fb40b2fb1b4b7bd0b34b2bf7ff7194390542748977.png)\n\nFinally, connecting up the parts of the graph above, we get an approximate sketch of $f'(x)$:\n\n![](images/f2/f21fca7744d7196e457f0a9d4511f644a3cf1e8c61018c152fc74ea7f6999f3e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102004 #edb/ref 1065 641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102004 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "732e85fe-aec5-51f6-806c-68e476077346" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102004 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
